Jonadab the Unsightly One wrote:
> Janis Papanagnou <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:
>
>> Probabilistic facts like "Objects of type <XY> are
>> plentiful in Nethack" should be taken with a good
>> grain of salt, as I observed in my last game once
>> again. The scroll of enchant weapon is the third
>> most common random scroll (identify:18%, light:9%,
>> enchant weapon:8%) of all 22 scroll types (where
>> the five least common types have a probability of
>> just 1.5% each).
>
> The thing is, most of the items in the game do
> not come from random object generation.

Janis P got screwed by the RNG.  All of us can sympathize!

Random object generation goes by odds in the tables.  Have enough
objects created to overwhlem the RNG and you'll end up approaching
stacks that approach the odds in the tables.  The have this many objects
in the game takes pudding farming or maybe extinctionist games.

Janis P is now ascending sooner than such games and so she's (he's?  I
forget) becoming more and more subject to the whims of the RNG as the
games get shorter.  I say - Congratulations for having this problem at
all, combined with the been-there-done-that sympathy that so many of us
have shared.

In many games the less random object creation accounts for most of the
items.  Kill enough elves and hobbits and you're going to have a big
stack of elven daggers.  And so on for many object types.  it's
something we all experience but not relevant to object types not created
in initial inventories.

> Admittedly, getting almost to the Wizard before
> encountering any scrolls of enchant weapon at
> all is quite a bit more spare than I have personally
> experienced.  However, I've had games where I
> had to go into Gehennom with a +2 or +3 main
> weapon and only +0 projectiles.

It seems like most games are outliers of one sort or another.  No altars
but the guaranteed ones, never a scroll of genocide, you name it.  It's
in the nature of the beast.

> I do tend to get some enchant weapon if I polypile
> scrolls.  That, of course, requires a source of
> polymorph, which not all games give you unless
> you spend a wish on it; and also, polypiling is a
> bit tedious and can cause pet maintenance issues,
> especially if you're trying to keep an herbivore.

Yes.  With the caveat that polypile reduces total number of objects.  In
the case of potions I tend to convert enough to holy water or alchemize
them to gain ability, gain level, full healing so I often don't have a
lot of spares.  Not so with scrolls, but that's in highly prepared
games.  Janis is out of the phase of highly prepared games.


